---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { FREE_TO_RUN_LINE, LICENSE_CLAUSE } from '../../data/product-facts.mjs';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'Install mod_pagespeed on cPanel / EasyApache 4',
  description:
    'Signed EA4 RPMs for mod_pagespeed 1.15 on EL8 + EL9 (AlmaLinux, Rocky, RHEL, CloudLinux) + EasyApache 4. Drop a yum repo file, dnf install, tick the Apache Modules checkbox in WHM, rebuild Apache.',
  about: 'mod_pagespeed 1.15 on cPanel / EasyApache 4',
  image: 'https://modpagespeed.com/og-default.png?v=2',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'We-Amp B.V.' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/logo.svg',
    },
  },
  url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/1.1/cpanel/',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="mod_pagespeed for cPanel / EasyApache 4 — Install Guide"
  description="Install mod_pagespeed 1.15 on cPanel / EasyApache 4 — signed EA4 RPMs for EL8 + EL9 (AlmaLinux, Rocky, RHEL, CloudLinux), maintained by We-Amp. One yum repo, one dnf install, tick the WHM box."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-bg-secondary border-b border-border">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-20">
      <p class="eyebrow text-interactive">mod_pagespeed 1.15 · cPanel / EA4</p>
      <h1 class="h1-hero mt-3 text-text-body">
        mod_pagespeed for cPanel / EasyApache 4
      </h1>
      <p class="lede mt-6">
        Actively maintained since 2024. Signed RPMs from We-Amp, served out of
        <code class="font-mono">packages.modpagespeed.com</code>. Drops into EasyApache 4 as
        <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code> — same package name the
        <a
          href="https://github.com/apache/incubator-pagespeed-cpanel"
          class="text-interactive underline"
          rel="noopener">apache/incubator-pagespeed-cpanel</a
        > repo used before it was archived in 2023. We picked up the upstream and kept shipping; the SRPM
        in this repo is the canonical EA4 build today.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- One-line install -->
  <section id="install" class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Install</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      EasyApache 4 ships only on RHEL-family hosts. Write the yum repo file, then
      <code class="font-mono">dnf install</code> the module:
    </p>

    <div
      class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-bg-secondary p-6"
    >
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-text-annotation">
        1. Write the yum repo file
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-muted">
        On EL8 (AlmaLinux 8, Rocky 8, CloudLinux 8) and EL9 (AlmaLinux 9, Rocky 9, RHEL 9,
        CloudLinux 9) hosts — <code class="font-mono">$releasever</code> selects the right tree:
      </p>
      <pre
        class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code data-umami-event="cta_cpanel_ea4_repo_stanza">sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/modpagespeed-ea4.repo &gt;/dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
[ea4]
name=mod_pagespeed for EasyApache 4
baseurl=https://packages.modpagespeed.com/yum/ea4/el$releasever/x86_64/
enabled=0
gpgcheck=1
gpgkey=https://packages.modpagespeed.com/pubkey.gpg
EOF</code></pre>

      <p
        class="mt-6 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-text-annotation"
      >
        2. Install the module
      </p>
      <pre
        class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code data-umami-event="cta_cpanel_ea4_dnf_install">sudo dnf install --enablerepo=ea4 ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code></pre>

      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-text-muted">
        <code class="font-mono">enabled=0</code> + <code class="font-mono">--enablerepo=ea4</code> keeps
        the EA4 RPM tree out of routine <code class="font-mono">dnf update</code> runs unless you ask
        for it. The package is signed with key <code class="font-mono"
          >rsa4096/F50D6054F10712A0</code
        > — same key as the rest of <code class="font-mono">packages.modpagespeed.com</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-text-annotation">
      Today the operator authors <code class="font-mono">modpagespeed-ea4.repo</code> by hand; the existing
      <a href="/download/apt-yum/" class="text-interactive underline"
        ><code class="font-mono">install.sh</code></a
      > targets the stock apt + yum trees only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- After install: WHM Customize UI -->
  <section
    class="bg-bg-secondary border-y border-border py-16"
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">
        After install — WHM Customize UI
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
        The RPM drops a module file but does not flip it on. Apache only loads
        <code class="font-mono">mod_pagespeed</code> after the operator ticks the box in WHM and rebuilds.
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-text-body list-decimal pl-6">
        <li>
          WHM → <em>Software</em> → <em>EasyApache 4</em> → <em>Customize</em> on the current profile.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open the <em>Apache Modules</em> step. <code class="font-mono">mod_pagespeed</code> appears
          alongside the other EA4 modules.
        </li>
        <li>Tick the checkbox. <em>Review</em>, then <em>Provision</em>. EA4 rebuilds Apache.</li>
        <li>
          When the provision finishes, the module is loaded and the
          <a href="/features/" class="text-interactive underline">filter set</a> runs on every vhost
          served by <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-httpd</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-text-annotation">
        Prefer the command line? On a fresh provision the module is also wired into
        <code class="font-mono">/etc/apache2/conf.d/modpagespeed.conf</code>. Edit that file (or a
        per-vhost include) the same way you would on any RHEL Apache host — see the
        <a href="/1.1/docs/getting-started/" class="text-interactive underline"
          >getting-started guide</a
        > for the directive reference.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin console -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Admin console</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      Install and run mod_pagespeed free — it fully optimizes out of the box. The global admin
      console lives at the same path on every server <a href="/1.1/" class="text-interactive underline"
        >mod_pagespeed 1.15</a
      > supports — Apache, nginx, IIS, and experimental Envoy:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code>https://your-host.example.com/pagespeed_global_admin</code></pre>

    <ol class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-text-body list-decimal pl-6">
      <li>
        Open <code class="font-mono">/pagespeed_global_admin</code> on any vhost. The admin route is global
        to the server, not scoped to one vhost; <code class="font-mono">/pagespeed_admin/</code>
        is the per-vhost statistics endpoint.
      </li>
    </ol>


    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-annotation">
      The software is free on every site and host. Hosting providers running it at fleet scale can
      join the <a href="/hosting-partners/" class="text-interactive underline">hosting partner
      program</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- cpanel/elevate warning -->
  <section
    class="bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border-y border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/50 py-16"
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300">
        Operational warning
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">
        Remove before <code class="font-mono">cpanel/elevate</code>. Reinstall after.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-amber-900/90 dark:text-amber-100/90">
        Any third-party EA4 module — <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code>
        included — blocks <code class="font-mono">cpanel/elevate</code> on an OS-major upgrade (EL8 →
        EL9, EL9 → EL10). This is the same precedent <code class="font-mono">mod_passenger</code>
        sets. cPanel's preflight stops on the package; the upgrade does not start. We build for both EL8
        and EL9, so an EL8 → EL9 upgrade has a target-OS package to reinstall afterwards.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-amber-900/90 dark:text-amber-100/90">
        Three-step runbook on the OS upgrade window:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-amber-900/90 dark:text-amber-100/90 list-decimal pl-6">
        <li>
          <code class="font-mono">sudo dnf remove ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code> — the module unwires
          and Apache rebuilds without it. Any
          <code class="font-mono">/etc/apache2/conf.d/modpagespeed.conf</code> stays on disk.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run <code class="font-mono">/scripts/elevate-cpanel</code> through the OS upgrade. EA4 itself
          migrates to the new EL release.
        </li>
        <li>
          Re-enable the yum repo for the new EL release (<code class="font-mono"
            >baseurl=…/yum/ea4/el10/x86_64/</code
          >, once EL10 is available) and reinstall the module:
          <code class="font-mono">sudo dnf install --enablerepo=ea4 ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code
          >. The existing config is picked up unchanged.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-amber-800/90 dark:text-amber-300/90">
        The post-install script also writes a one-line reminder to
        <code class="font-mono">/etc/motd</code> so it surfaces at the next SSH session before the upgrade
        window.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Licensing -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Licensing</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      Free &mdash; the maintained continuation is {LICENSE_CLAUSE}, in development and in
      production, on any number of sites or hosts. What We-Amp sells is <a
        href="/pricing/"
        class="text-interactive underline">support &mdash; and hardened builds</a
      >; hosting providers can join the
      <a href="/hosting-partners/" class="text-interactive underline">partner program</a>. Support
      terms:
      <a href="https://we-amp.com/licensing/" class="text-interactive underline" rel="noopener"
        >we-amp.com/licensing</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6">
      <a
        href="/pricing/"
        data-umami-event="cta_cpanel_ea4_pricing"
        class="text-sm font-semibold text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover inline-flex items-center gap-2"
      >
        See full pricing
        <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Not supported -->
  <section
    class="bg-bg-secondary border-y border-border py-16"
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Not yet supported</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
        Current limits of the cPanel/EA4 channel:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-text-body">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span
            ><strong>EL10.</strong> Waiting on EA4 itself to ship EL10. We'll add the
            <code class="font-mono">el10</code> tree to the repo once
            <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-httpd</code> publishes there.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span
            ><strong>arm64.</strong> EA4 RPMs ship x86_64 only at launch. arm64 isn't packaged yet;
            <a href="/contact/" class="text-interactive underline">contact us</a> if it blocks you.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span
            ><strong>Ubuntu EA4.</strong> cPanel's Ubuntu EA4 support is partial / preview. We ship RHEL-family
            only — EL8 and EL9 (AlmaLinux, Rocky, RHEL, CloudLinux).</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span
            ><strong>WHM plugin UI.</strong> Config still lives in
            <code class="font-mono">modpagespeed.conf</code> and per-vhost includes. There's no WHM plugin
            for point-and-click config yet.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-text-muted">
        The module is built against EA4's Apache ABI (Module Magic Number <code class="font-mono"
          >20120211</code
        >), which cPanel has held stable across the entire Apache 2.4 series. Routine
        <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24</code> updates keep the same MMN, so a
        <code class="font-mono">yum update</code> does not disturb the installed module. If a future Apache
        release ever changes the ABI, the package's <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-mmn</code>
        requirement makes <code class="font-mono">yum update</code> hold the Apache upgrade with a clear
        dependency message instead of loading an incompatible module, so your site keeps serving. We track
        every <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24</code> release and publish a matching signed rebuild
        when an ABI change requires one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Bottom CTA -->
  <section class="bg-stone-900">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="h2-section text-white">
        Maintained upstream. Signed RPMs. cPanel-native install.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        {FREE_TO_RUN_LINE}
        Hosting providers running it at fleet scale can join the
        <a href="/hosting-partners/" class="underline hover:text-stone-200">hosting partner program</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <a href="#install" class="btn-primary" data-umami-event="cta_cpanel_ea4_bottom_install"
          >Install &amp; run</a
        >
        <a
          href="/pricing/"
          class="rounded-md border border-stone-600 px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-stone-300 hover:bg-stone-800 transition-colors"
          data-umami-event="cta_cpanel_ea4_bottom_buy">Support plans</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- Trademark / non-affiliation disclaimer -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-12">
    <p class="text-xs text-text-annotation">
      cPanel, WHM, and EasyApache are trademarks of cPanel, L.L.C. We-Amp B.V. is not affiliated
      with or endorsed by cPanel, L.L.C.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
